# No-panic policy: every project, every language

The AIC PIM workspace ships **long-running services**, **elevated
installers**, **operator-facing GUIs**, and **headless background
daemons**. A single panic in any of those processes is a
customer-visible incident — not a "developer bug".

## The rule

> **Every recoverable failure must fail gracefully — log a clear
> explanation, attempt reasonable recovery, and let the program keep
> running.** Code that aborts the process on a runtime error is a bug.

1. **No new code may crash on a recoverable failure.** The default shape
   of every fallible call site is *propagate an error* — not *unwrap and
   abort*.
2. **Every error path logs enough to diagnose without a debugger** —
   operation, inputs (privacy-appropriate), underlying error text, and
   the recovery action taken.
3. **Every error path specifies recovery**, even if that means
   skip-and-continue, retry-with-backoff, fall-back-to-default, or
   surface-error-to-user-and-stay-running. "Crash and let the OS restart
   us" is **not** acceptable recovery.
4. **Tests are exempt from the constructs, not the design.** `unwrap` /
   `expect` are acceptable inside `#[cfg(test)]`, `tests/`,
   `**/*_tests.rs`, `*.spec.ts`, `test_*.py`, `*.Tests.ps1`.

Never "make it compile" by adding `.unwrap()`, `.expect(...)`, TypeScript
`!`, bare `try { … } catch {}`, or the equivalent. If a failure is
genuinely impossible by construction, say so explicitly with a per-site
allow and comment (see **Documented infallibility**).

## Forbidden constructs by language

### Rust (primary)

In `**/src/**/*.rs`, `build.rs`, `examples/**/*.rs`, outside test paths:

- `.unwrap()` / `.expect("...")` on a `Result` or `Option` when the error
  is reachable at runtime (I/O, network, parsing, deserialization,
  env-var lookup, lock acquisition, non-`'static` regex compile,
  conversion of user data, time math).
- `panic!`, `unreachable!`, `todo!`, `unimplemented!` on a reachable path.
- `assert!` / `debug_assert!` / `assert_eq!` for **runtime** validation of
  external input. Failed validation returns a typed error, never aborts.
- `unwrap_or_else(|_| panic!(...))` and other closures that still panic.
- `Box::leak` / `mem::forget` of resources that would otherwise propagate
  a Drop error.

Use instead: `?` plus `anyhow::Context::{context, with_context}` so the
chain says **what** was being attempted; a typed `thiserror` enum at
module / crate boundaries (`anyhow` for binaries and the very top of the
stack); for locks, match on `lock()` and on poison, log at
`tracing::error`, replace or release the data, return a typed error; for
retries, bounded exponential backoff with a documented attempt cap, every
retry logged, a final error surfaced; for `main`, catch the error from
`try_main()`, log the chain with `tracing::error`, and
`std::process::exit(<code>)` — never let a panic escape `main`.

Required Cargo lints in every crate (workspace root
`[workspace.lints.clippy]` or per-crate `[lints.clippy]`):

```toml
[lints.clippy]
unwrap_used = "deny"
expect_used = "deny"
panic = "deny"
todo = "deny"
unimplemented = "deny"
unreachable = "deny"
indexing_slicing = "warn"  # surface bounds-check panics for review
```

Test exemption at the top of `lib.rs` / `main.rs`:

```rust
#![cfg_attr(test, allow(clippy::unwrap_used, clippy::expect_used, clippy::panic))]
```

…and `#![allow(clippy::unwrap_used, clippy::expect_used, clippy::panic)]`
at the top of each file under `tests/`.

### TypeScript / JavaScript (Tauri front-ends, web UI, ui-kit)

Forbidden in `**/src/**/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx}`: the `!` non-null assertion on
runtime values (DOM queries, JSON parses, fetch responses, IPC payloads);
`as` casts that bypass `unknown` narrowing without a validator;
`JSON.parse` with no `try`/`catch` and fallback; `throw` from a top-level
effect (event handler, IPC callback, render path) with no boundary that
catches and logs; `Promise` chains that drop rejections.

Use instead: a schema validator (`zod`, `valibot`, `io-ts`) for JSON
crossing a trust boundary; React error boundaries on every screen root
with toast-and-log; every `invoke(...)` wrapped in `try`/`catch` that
logs, surfaces a user-facing message, and leaves the UI usable.

### Python, PowerShell, C++ / Win32 / Slint

- **Python** — never `assert` a runtime condition (`-O` strips it); never
  `raise` from a top-level callback without a logged-and-recovered
  handler; no `dict[key]` on user input without `.get()`, no bare
  `int(...)` without `ValueError` handling.
- **PowerShell** — `$ErrorActionPreference = "Stop"` is fine, but wrap
  long scripts in `try { … } catch { Write-Error / Write-Warning; exit N }`
  so the operator gets a message, not a stack trace. Never `throw` from a
  nested helper with no higher-level catch. Always `Test-Path` before
  `Get-Item` / `Copy-Item -LiteralPath` on user-derived paths.
- **C++ / Win32 / Slint** — error returns or `std::expected` /
  `std::optional`, never exceptions escaping the message loop. Wrap any
  Rust callback body invoked by the OS in `std::panic::catch_unwind` and
  log + return a safe default on catch.

## Behavioural requirements at runtime

A panic-free codebase is necessary but not sufficient. Every error path
must be:

1. **Logged** — `tracing` (Rust), `console.error` + front-end logger (TS),
   `logging` (Python), `Write-Error` / event log (PowerShell), with
   enough context to diagnose without source access.
2. **Bounded** — retries have an attempt cap, polling has backoff,
   reconnects have a maximum interval. No path spins silently.
3. **Recovered** — state the recovery explicitly: skip-and-continue,
   fall-back-to-default, restart-this-task-not-the-process, prompt-user,
   degrade-feature. Comment it when non-obvious.
4. **Visible** — long-running daemons report degraded health (see
   `runtime_status.json`) so operators can see recovery happening.

## Documented infallibility (when `expect` is genuinely OK)

Some calls really cannot fail: `Mutex::lock()` immediately after creating
the mutex in the same function; `NonZeroUsize::new(CONST_NONZERO)`;
`Regex::new(STATIC_STR)` where the test suite verifies it compiles;
`OnceCell::get()` after a verified `set()` in the same scope;
`HmacSha256::new_from_slice(secret)` on a known-correct length.

An `expect` is acceptable there **only if** it is annotated with
`#[allow(clippy::expect_used)]` (or `unwrap_used`) at the call site and
carries a comment naming the panic-policy category: **B** infallible by
construction, **C** documented domain invariant, **D** guard-then-use.

```rust
// B: regex is a &'static str; compiled at module-load by the static initializer
#[allow(clippy::expect_used)]
static EMAIL: Lazy<Regex> = Lazy::new(|| {
    Regex::new(r"^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$").expect("hard-coded regex is valid")
});
```

Default to **not** using these escape hatches.

## Required agent behavior

1. **Identify each fallible call** and write its error path in the same
   edit. Never commit a `.unwrap()` "to be handled later".
2. **Wire the error to a log site** with operation name, inputs, and
   underlying error. Default level: `warn` for transient, `error` for
   definitive.
3. **Specify the recovery** in code or a comment naming the strategy.
4. **If editing code that already panics**, treat it as debt to fix
   opportunistically — surface it in your reply and either fix it inline
   or note it for follow-up. Never copy a bad pattern forward.
5. **If the user explicitly asks for a panic**, accept it once they have
   said so — but still log the reason via `tracing::error!` and, in a
   daemon, prefer `std::process::exit(N)` after logging so the OS / SCM
   sees a clean exit code.

Per-repo lint-table adoption status:
`core-assets/docs/dev/rule-enforcement-status.md`. When you touch a repo
whose Cargo / package config is missing the lint table, add it as part of
that change set rather than leaving the gap.

## Why `alwaysApply`

Push-triggered CI was deliberately removed
([`ci-policy.mdc`](ci-policy.mdc)). The agent and the per-crate clippy
lint table are the only two enforcement surfaces, so this rule must be
loaded in every conversation — the first instinct must be to write the
recovery path, not to be rescued by a CI check that no longer runs.
